@kunchenguid — ready for review whenever you have a moment.

Short version: the instruction to keep the internal role vocabulary out of published text used to say "anything that leaves the fleet", which unintentionally collided with the deliberate public-reply voice in `fmx-respond`. This narrows it to name the actual published-artifact surfaces — commit messages, pull request titles and bodies, review comments, issues, committed files — and leaves the `fmx-respond` Voice section untouched.

It also moves the rule into the generated briefs themselves, before the task, so a worker is told once up front rather than depending on it being remembered per task. Tests cover all three brief variants and the ordering.

Orca is now a good first concrete backend for this contract rather than only an example. The current CLI exposes supervised-worker state, while the broader versioned protocol discussion is active upstream:

- Firstmate adoption plan: #1813
- Orca protocol discussion: https://github.com/stablyai/orca/issues/9810
- compatibility/restart suite: #2084

A bounded first slice could add an optional backend capability such as `wait_events(cursor, timeout)` while leaving the existing watcher polling path unchanged:

1. capability-probe the Orca supervised-worker/event surface;
2. persist a per-task attempt identity and event cursor;
3. normalize only authoritative Orca transitions into the proposed envelope;
4. append/durably acknowledge before advancing the cursor;
5. fall back visibly to polling when the capability or event stream is unavailable;
6. periodically reconcile authoritative worker/endpoint state even when push looks healthy.

That would let the contract prove itself against one real consumer before generalizing every harness/backend. The minimum useful acceptance test is blocked + completed + failed delivery, restart replay exactly once, duplicate-event dedupe, and a visible diagnostic naming typed-events versus polling fallback.

Confirmed another live occurrence on devbox with a Pi primary on Herdr 0.8.0, and the impact extends beyond a misleading `fm-send` exit code.

The away-mode supervisor used the same Herdr submission path and logged repeated:

```text
inject failed: submit unconfirmed after 3 retries (verdict=pending, text may be in composer)
```

The exact escalation had already reached and been handled by the Pi session, but the false-negative acknowledgement left the buffered escalation live. It then repeatedly delivered the same resolved event. After a guarded away-mode restart, the same behavior repeated for `check: rearm-resurface`, producing a visible flood of duplicate operational messages until away mode was stopped.

Observed safeguards and boundaries:

- the message text was not intentionally retyped by the supervisor;
- the duplicate deliveries followed the unconfirmed `pending` verdict despite successful receipt;
- both primary and secondmate watcher processes remained otherwise healthy;
- normal non-away supervision was healthy after the guarded stop;
- no durable work event was lost.

This raises the severity of the false-negative: callers that retain a buffer until confirmed can turn one successfully delivered message into repeated user-visible escalations. The visible `rearm-resurface` flood overlaps with #2615, but this occurrence's direct evidence points to Herdr/Pi submit acknowledgement and buffer retirement rather than multiple watcher-side publications.

